            0.635,
            0.042,
            0.848,
            0.058
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How Forces Affect Motion"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 99"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.087,
            0.629,
            0.24
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "vanishes and the force applied by the stretched rubber band in the forward direction on the stack of coins sets it moving. The coins start moving means that the force applied by the rubber band is larger than the force of friction and a net force acts on stack of coins in the forward direction (Fig. 6.13a). Due to the net force, the velocity of stack of coins changes from zero to a certain value, i.e., the net force provides an acceleration in the forward direction."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.243,
            0.628,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "However, the moment the stack of coins loses contact with the rubber band, the force due to the rubber band is no longer acting upon it. But the force of friction continues to act upon the stack of coins in the direction opposite to their motion (Fig. 6.13b). It gradually decreases its velocity and finally brings it to rest."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.662,
            0.091,
            0.915,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.662,
                0.091,
                0.915,
                0.262
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.645,
                0.265,
                0.935,
                0.322
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 6.13: Force acting on the stack of coins (a) due to the stretched rubber band and friction upon release of rubber band, and (b) only due to friction"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.348,
            0.776,
            0.433
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Even though the rubber band is stretched by the same amount in each case, the distance travelled by the stack of coins on different surfaces changes. This indicates that the force of friction on these surfaces is different. But how can you check if the force of friction is indeed different for different surfaces?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.442,
            0.399,
            0.46
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 6.2: Let us measure"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.463,
            0.536,
            0.481
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Take a spring balance and a wooden block."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.483,
            0.774,
            0.537
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Place the spring balance in a horizontal position on one of the surfaces used in Activity 6.1 and check that its scale reading is zero. Attach the wooden block to the hook of the spring balance as shown in Fig. 6.14."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.541,
            0.733,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.165,
                0.541,
                0.733,
                0.631
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_10.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.273,
                0.638,
                0.631,
                0.654
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 6.14: A block being pulled by a spring balance"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.661,
            0.775,
            0.796
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Pull the spring balance with gradually increasing force and note down the reading on it when the block just starts moving. What does this reading indicate? The forces acting on the block are the force applied by the spring on it and the force of friction. If the velocity of the block is neither increasing nor decreasing, what can you say about the net force acting on the block? Does the reading of the spring balance indicate the magnitude of the force of friction acting on the wooden block?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.8,
            0.759,
            0.818
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Now repeat step 3 on the remaining three surfaces from Activity 6.1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.82,
            0.775,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Compare",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the readings of the spring balance for all surfaces. Are the readings different? Is the reading smallest for the surface on which the stack of coins travelled the largest distance? Is the reading largest for which the distance travelled was the smallest?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.891,
            0.775,
            0.927
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The reading of the spring balance gives an approximate measure of the force of friction acting between the surface of the block and the surface"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
